WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:10.260
Pipelines often include variable values: file paths, search terms, service names, environment labels, report names, branch names, and patterns.

2
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:13.560
Those values should be treated as data first.

3
00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:20.140
If a value is meant to be literal, it should not accidentally become shell syntax, a glob pattern, or multiple arguments.

4
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:22.980
Quoting helps preserve argument boundaries.

5
00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:25.800
A path with spaces should remain one path.

6
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:28.140
A branch name should remain data.

7
00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:33.280
A report name should not become a pattern unless the script intentionally supports pattern behavior.

8
00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:37.860
When values enter pipeline commands, the script should make their role clear.

9
00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.660
Validation decides which values the pipeline supports.

10
00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.440
Paths, names, filter values, and modes should be checked before they influence command behavior.

11
00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:52.820
If a pipeline supports a limited set of filters or report types, allowlist those options.

12
00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:56.920
If a value is unsupported, reject it before the pipeline runs.

13
00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.400
Avoid building command strings from uncontrolled input.

14
00:01:00.500 --> 00:01:06.260
If the pipeline needs optional arguments, build them as structured arguments where possible.

15
00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:12.680
If the pipeline needs a filter value, define the safe form of that value and pass it as data.

16
00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:17.420
This keeps pipeline behavior reviewable and reduces accidental interpretation.

17
00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.180
Filenames and metadata deserve the same care as direct user input.

18
00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:29.880
They may come from tools, repositories, storage systems, or CI/CD platforms, but they can still be unexpected.

19
00:01:29.980 --> 00:01:35.020
A secure pipeline treats those values as data crossing a boundary before they shape execution.
